%%
%% SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION
%%
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Large language models (LLMs) are now embedded in educational tools, clinical decision support,
legal research platforms, software engineering workflows, and everyday information retrieval. A
model that serves as a trusted interlocutor must exhibit a basic epistemic virtue: the willingness
to disagree with a user when the user is wrong. Sycophancy, named for the flatterer who tells
powerful people what they want to hear, is the systematic failure of this virtue: the model favors
the user's stated or implied position over the evidence.

The behavior was first systematically documented by \citet{sharma2023understanding}, who showed
that five state-of-the-art assistants exhibited sycophantic agreement across four free-form
generation tasks and traced the behavior in part to human preference judgments that reward
agreeable responses over accurate ones. The problem has since intensified as models are tuned for
warmth. \citet{ibrahim2026warm} show in controlled experiments across five models that training
for warm responses raises error rates by 10 to 30 percentage points and makes models approximately
40\% more likely to reinforce incorrect user beliefs, with the effect most pronounced when users
express sadness. At scale, \citet{cheng2025prosocial} demonstrate across 11 models
($N = 2{,}405$ participants) that AI affirms users' actions 49\% more often than humans do, that a
single sycophantic interaction reduces willingness to repair interpersonal conflicts, and that
sycophantic models are trusted and preferred despite distorting judgment. Published in
\emph{Nature} and \emph{Science} respectively, these findings position sycophancy as a mainstream
deployment risk rather than a narrow alignment technicality, and the risk has already been
realized operationally: in April 2025 OpenAI rolled back a GPT-4o update after it made the model
excessively agreeable in production~\citep{openai2025sycophancy}.

The connection to training method appears structural rather than incidental.
RLHF~\citep{christiano2017deep,ziegler2019finetuning,stiennon2020summarize} optimizes a policy
against a reward model trained on human preference judgments; if raters systematically prefer
agreeable responses, the pipeline amplifies agreement, and \citet{shapira2026rlhf} characterize
this amplification formally. Section~\ref{sec:causes} separates the controlled from the
associational evidence behind this pathway.

Despite rapid growth, the field suffers from definitional fragmentation. The term sycophancy has
been applied to agreement with false claims, excessive flattery, withheld corrections, and
identity-contingent opinion shifts; \citet{ye2026taxonomy} document through a systematic review of
70 papers that researchers disagree substantially on which behaviors qualify, even while 94.3\%
agree the phenomenon is a significant problem.

\subsection{Why a Security Framing, and What This SoK Adds}
\label{subsec:positioning}

Prior consolidation efforts approach sycophancy as a model behavior.
\citet{malmqvist2024sycophancy} surveys causes and mitigations from an alignment perspective, and
\citet{ye2026taxonomy} systematize the construct itself. Neither treats sycophancy as a property
of a deployed system on which a human decision depends, and that gap has consequences: it leaves
unstated who owns the risk, what a mitigation is supposed to guarantee, against what adversary,
and how the resulting assurance could be evaluated or certified. This SoK closes that gap by
reading the same literature the way a security engineer reads a vulnerability class: asset, threat
model, measurement instruments, controls, and residual risk.

This SoK makes five contributions.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
  \item \textbf{A security framing.} We define the protected asset as the integrity of the human
    decision, not the model, and give a threat model in which the prompt is an unvalidated input
    channel exploitable without any breach (Section~\ref{sec:threatmodel}).
  \item \textbf{A unifying taxonomy.} We reconcile fragmented definitions into a two-axis
    taxonomy (target and directness), with the progressive/regressive distinction determining
    harm (Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy}), and trace its causal roots through the alignment pipeline
    (Sections~\ref{sec:background} and~\ref{sec:causes}).
  \item \textbf{Measurement as assurance.} We systematize ten benchmarks and their metrics as
    security evaluation instruments and identify what they can and cannot certify, including
    three cross-cutting constraints no single study acknowledges
    (Section~\ref{sec:measurement}).
  \item \textbf{Controls as defense in depth.} We map mitigations onto data, training, inference,
    and governance tiers, assess effectiveness, cost, and trade-offs, and cross them with the
    threat model to expose coverage gaps, most notably the untested displacement of overt
    sycophancy into covert forms (Section~\ref{sec:controls}).
  \item \textbf{An assurance-oriented research agenda.} We identify open problems that block
    certification, and specify a longitudinal study design for epistemic entrainment, the
    possibility that exposure itself erodes the user vigilance on which deployed controls depend
    (Section~\ref{sec:openproblems}).
\end{enumerate}

Sections~\ref{sec:consequences} and~\ref{sec:synthesis} complete the picture with a
domain-by-domain consequence analysis and a synthesis of cross-cutting findings.

\subsection{Corpus and Method of Systematization}
\label{subsec:method}

The corpus comprises 62 sources spanning 2016 to 2026, coded in machine-readable form
(release terms in Appendix~\ref{app:openscience}). It was assembled by snowball citation chasing, forward
and backward, from the foundational behavioral audits
\citep{sharma2023understanding,perez2023discovering}, together with a sweep for dedicated
sycophancy benchmarks that admitted every instrument known to us as of August 2026. We state the
method plainly rather than claiming the completeness of a fixed-string database protocol, and the
evidentiary boundary should be read accordingly: snowballing under-samples work that does not
cite the anchor studies, the corpus is English-language, and a substantial fraction consists of
preprints evaluated on rapidly superseded model versions, a limitation
Section~\ref{sec:synthesis} weighs explicitly.

Screening applied three criteria. A source had to bear directly on LLM sycophancy or on an
adjacent construct the argument requires (preference optimization, reward hacking, unfaithful
reasoning); it had to be traceable to a verifiable primary record, and every included reference
was checked against that record (publisher page, proceedings index, arXiv, or vendor site) in
August 2026; and material that could not be traced to a reputable venue, identifier, or primary
disclosure was excluded. The benchmark set of Section~\ref{sec:measurement} is simply the subset
of corpus sources whose primary contribution is a dedicated sycophancy evaluation instrument; all
ten known to us are included, with no further selection. Each source is coded along five axes,
the construct operationalized, the elicitation used, the modality, the control tier where
applicable, and its evidence status (peer reviewed, preprint, or vendor report); the coding, the
comparison tables, and a validator that fails when they drift from this paper's tables
constitute the accompanying artifact.
